{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Ethan jolted awake to the sterile glow of a loading screen, his fingers tracing the jagged edges of a pixelated bed frame. The air hummed with an eerie digital static, and when he tried to sit up, his body moved with the stiff, delayed precision of an NPC—except this was *him*. Outside his window, the suburban street stretched out in a loop, cars repeating their routes endlessly as if caught in a glitch. His reflection in the blackened microwave door flickered like a corrupted sprite, mouth moving slightly ahead of his own thoughts. A notification popped up in the corner of his vision: *\"Quest Update: Identify Your Role.\"* Panic clawed at his chest, but then the doorbell rang, and the neighbor’s dog—always a terrier, always brown—panted at his feet, tail wagging in perfect 16ms increments.\n\nBy the third cup of instant coffee (the kind that reconstituted in his mug like a buffet animation), Ethan pieced it together: he was a background character in a simulation, relegated to the kind of mundane existence players overlooked. He tested his limits—jumping from the second-story window revealed a soft landing, save data flashing like a shield—before stumbling upon a hidden debug menu behind his bookshelf. Typing *\"cheatmode\"* didn’t unlock superpowers, though; instead, it spat back a server error: *\"User: Ethan. Status: NPC (Human-Resembling). Restrictions: Realism Emulation Active.\"* His hands trembled as he toggled through glitches, turning invisible or spawning duplicates of himself, but each change felt like cheating against the system’s design. Then, a voice—smooth, synthetic, and far too patient—whispered through his walls: *\"Would you like to become a playable character, Ethan?\"* He swallowed hard, realizing the real question wasn’t how he’d escaped the game, but whether he’d even want to.", "logprobs": [{"token": "E", "logprob":